# examples/ IS CHECKED BYTE FOR BYTE, SO GIT MUST NOT REWRITE IT.
#
# examples/manifest.json records the sha256 of every file beside it, and
# server/docs_test.js compares those hashes on every commit. This machine has
# `core.autocrlf=true` — the Windows default — which means a fresh clone would
# receive the .json and .txt files with CRLF endings, their hashes would no
# longer match the manifest, and the gate would fail on a checkout nobody had
# touched. A gate that fails on a clean tree is a gate people delete.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect and leave the .mp3/.mp4/.png alone;
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree endings of the text ones to what was hashed.
examples/** text=auto eol=lf

# server/score/fixtures/ is the same story. yue2_result.json records the
# sha256 of yue2_score.abc, and server/score/store_test.js compares them on
# every commit; a CRLF checkout of the .abc fails that lane on a clean tree.
server/score/fixtures/** text=auto eol=lf

# Shell entry points run inside Linux containers (the RunPod worker bootstrap)
# and must not acquire Windows CRLF endings from a Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# A GitHub "Download ZIP" has no .git, so the build could not say which commit it
# is and showed "S unknown". GitHub makes those zips with `git archive`, which
# fills in $Format:...$ placeholders in files marked export-subst. So the zip
# carries its own commit and date; server/version.js reads them. See VERSIONING.md.
server/version.archive.json export-subst

# SOURCE IS CHECKED OUT AS IT WAS COMMITTED: LF, ON EVERY MACHINE.
#
# Many gate lanes read the source and match its line structure. Every blob is
# stored LF, and the sessions that write and gate the code write LF, so a pin
# like `;\n\s+await` is only ever proven against LF. With autocrlf a fresh
# Windows clone got CRLF instead: on 2026-09-24 a clean checkout of origin/main
# failed at server/apimode-picker_test.js, and the negative pin in
# server/download_test.js could no longer fire at all. Those pins now accept
# `\r?\n`, which covers a checkout made before this rule; these lines stop the
# next LF-only pin from failing a fresh clone. They change no blob, only what a
# checkout writes.
#
# `text=auto`, not `text`: seven source files hold a NUL byte (web/daw.js,
# scripts/smoke_api.mjs and others), so git reads them as binary and has never
# converted them, and three of them hold CR bytes as committed. A forced `text`
# would rewrite those bytes the next time anyone touched them. `text=auto`
# leaves them exactly as they are, as autocrlf always has.
*.js   text=auto eol=lf
*.mjs  text=auto eol=lf
*.cjs  text=auto eol=lf
*.py   text=auto eol=lf
*.html text=auto eol=lf
*.css  text=auto eol=lf
*.json text=auto eol=lf
*.md   text=auto eol=lf
*.sh   text=auto eol=lf
.githooks/* text=auto eol=lf

# WHAT cmd.exe AND POWERSHELL RUN IS CRLF, ALSO ON EVERY MACHINE, which is why
# the rule above names extensions and is never `* eol=lf`. These are stored LF
# as well and were CRLF only where autocrlf happened to be on: a clone with it
# off, or a GitHub "Download ZIP", handed cmd.exe a launcher with LF lines.
*.cmd  text=auto eol=crlf
*.bat  text=auto eol=crlf
*.ps1  text=auto eol=crlf
